//
// Probe: the `math.normalize` and `math.smoothing` forms that `coverage`
// reported as implemented and run by no golden — the `.` workbench forms and
// the `,` keep forms.
//
// **A LIST reaches the workbench whole**, which is what makes these cheap to
// probe: `[ 1 2 3 ] { true } ?.` moves the list, and the reader takes it in
// one piece. An earlier reading of this gap assumed a `?.` per value would be
// needed, which is what a *drained* workbench would have required.
//
// **The `.` forms leave their answer on the workbench**, not the stack, so
// the workbench dump below is where the results are. The `,` forms keep the
// list they read, so each leaves two values rather than one.
//
"probe: math.normalize. reads the workbench and answers there" println
[ 1 2 3 ] { true } ?. math.normalize.

"probe: math.normalize., keeps the list it read, so two values remain" println
[ 1 2 3 ] { true } ?. math.normalize.,

"probe: math.smoothing, keeps its list on the stack" println
[ 1 2 3 4 ] math.smoothing,

"probe: math.smoothing. and math.smoothing., on the workbench" println
[ 1 2 3 4 ] { true } ?. math.smoothing.
[ 1 2 3 4 ] { true } ?. math.smoothing.,
